58:A common time series foundation model (TSFM) recipe pairs a shared, frozen backbone with a task-specific head. Because downstream series mix heterogeneous regimes that benefit from different aggregation topologies, the natural upgrade is a mixture-of-experts (MoE) head: a router over architecturally distinct experts, one selected per input window. We identify a failure mode of this upgrade that arises with instance normalization. Once any backbone layer is unfrozen, the router converges to a one-hot assignment, routing entropy collapses to $0.000$, and only one expert ever receives a gradient update. We characterize and name this failure mode \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}. The mechanism is direct: instance-normalization layers (e.g., RevIN) strip the per-window mean and variance the router needs. A $720$-run sweep across standard MoE rescue mechanisms (load balancing, z-loss, entropy regularization, ReLU routing, expert-choice) converges to the same collapsed state, recovering at most $10.9\%$ MSE. This remains $2.7\times$ worse than our causal intervention, because the failure is in the router's input, not its optimization. We formalize the mechanism via a mutual-information decomposition. The resulting signal-ratio statistic, computable before training, predicts dataset vulnerability to collapse (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$) and correctly flags boundary cases. The diagnosis prescribes a one-line \emph{causal intervention}: \emph{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters} (RR-MoA) routes on the raw, pre-normalization input. RR-MoA sets a new SOTA for adapter-based TSFM transfer under a strictly frozen-backbone protocol: it wins all $54/54$ configurations on the primary backbone (6 datasets $\times$ 3 freeze levels $\times$ 3 seeds, $26$--$79\%$ MSE improvement over the best fixed adapter), strictly beats LoRA, TRACE, AdaMix, and full fine-tuning at $p{<}0.001$, and generalizes across four further backbones and an imputation task. Eight causal controls including a vision-modality replication isolate the cause to instance normalization, and the diagnosis predicts a \emph{Frozen Paradox}: frozen RR-MoA beats full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$ — a two-factor gap dominated by the MoE pool's architectural advantage ($26$--$79\%$ at matched freeze level), with a smaller within-architecture freeze effect ($\leq 13\%$, frozen best on $4/6$) causally attributable to gradient co-adaptation (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} + RDGF + RevIN-disabled full-FT). Applying the same raw-signal principle at the expert level (Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}) closes the gap on $5/6$ LTSF datasets at $H{=}96$ ($107/123$ cells across six backbones $\times$ four horizons).
76:\item We propose RR-MoA, the \emph{minimal causal intervention} that fixes the collapse: changing only the router's input, with no other change to the optimizer, the experts, or the backbone. Larger optimization-side interventions fail ($720$ runs, $\leq 10.9\%$ recovery, $2.7{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA), naive $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection fails ($+42$--$239\%$ MSE; $11/18$ cells re-collapse), and the signal-ratio predictor correctly flags datasets where raw routing should \emph{not} help (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$). A companion variant (Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}) extends the same principle to the expert level; together they close the gap to per-dataset supervised baselines while preserving frozen-backbone deployability.
389:\textbf{Datasets and tasks.} Six LTSF benchmarks (ETTh1/h2/m1/m2~\citep{zhou2021informer}, Weather, Electricity~\citep{godahewa2021monash}) with chronological splits and channel-wise normalization~\citep{nie2023patchtst}. We evaluate forecasting ($H{=}96$) and imputation ($20\%$ masked).
406:\caption{\textbf{Freeze-level ablation} (test MSE, mean$\pm$std, 3 seeds $\{42,43,44\}$, H=96). RR-MoA (sparse Top-2 routing over 5 experts) vs.\ the best single-head adapter from $\{$linear, attention, conv$\}$ trained with the same protocol. ``MSE $\Delta\%$ vs.\ single-head'' is the relative MSE change of RR-MoA over the single-head baseline; negative $=$ lower MSE $=$ better. RR-MoA wins \textbf{27/27} paired (cell, seed) comparisons in this table; combined with Appendix~\ref{app:extended_rrmoa} the score is \textbf{54/54} across 6 datasets $\times$ 3 freeze levels $\times$ 3 seeds. The gain is largest when frozen (``Frozen Paradox'').}
413:\multirow{3}{*}{ETTh1}
418:\multirow{3}{*}{ETTm1}
423:\multirow{3}{*}{Weather}
431:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} Frozen RR-MoA beats \emph{full fine-tuning} (all 8 blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 LRs) by $12$--$79\%$ on all 6 LTSF datasets (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:frozen_paradox}). \emph{This headline gap is a two-factor contrast} (frozen+MoE vs.\ unfrozen+single-head) that decomposes into two mechanisms with very different effect sizes:
433:\emph{(M2) Within-architecture freeze advantage (smaller, $\leq 13\%$, non-unidirectional).} Within RR-MoA itself (architecture held fixed), frozen is best on $4/6$ datasets; light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$ on the other two. The within-architecture freeze effect is therefore real but bounded and direction-mixed; the headline $12$--$79\%$ number is dominated by M1.
434:\emph{Causal evidence for M2 specifically.} Unfreezing triggers gradient co-adaptation (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}): the dominant expert reshapes the shared backbone, collapsing routing, and freezing prevents this loop and preserves expert diversity. The Router-Detached Gradient Flow ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}) unfreezes the backbone but cuts the routing-weighted gradient feedback, degrading MSE by $+35$--$100\%$ on three datasets — a direct counterfactual on the co-adaptation term. Disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers full-FT performance by $54$--$61\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), pinning the mechanism on instance normalization rather than optimization. SR-MoA (a different router architecture) independently replicates the within-architecture pattern (frozen best on $4/6$; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Even after $90$-config hyperparameter tuning (learning rates $10^{-5}$ to $10^{-6}$, $100$ epochs, cosine schedule with $10$-epoch warmup, layerwise LR decay, weight decay sweep, gradient accumulation up to effective batch $512$), full fine-tuning still loses by $51$--$71\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), ruling out optimization confounds.}
452:\multirow{4}{*}{ETTh1}
458:\multirow{4}{*}{ETTm1}
464:\multirow{4}{*}{Weather}
479:\caption{\textbf{Per-step routing / gradient trajectory} (AdaMix, ETTh1). (a)~Unfrozen+RevIN (red) collapses within ${\sim}50$ steps; strictly frozen control (blue) stays stable, isolating gradient co-adaptation from optimizer or data effects. (b)~Dominant expert's gradient grows while others starve (co-adaptation loop, \S\ref{sec:rrmoa}). Removing RevIN (separate experiment, Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}) shows the trajectory inverts.}
488:\caption{\textbf{Causal contrast: MOMENT vs Timer-XL} (ETTh1, last-4 unfreezing). Solid lines: unfrozen runs. MOMENT entropy collapses $0.51{\to}0.000$ in ${\sim}40$ steps (red); Timer-XL stays at ${\approx}1.60$ for all $400$ steps (orange). Dashed lines: frozen-backbone controls for both models, neither collapses. Dotted: $\log K{=}1.609$ ceiling. Same code, same data, only normalization differs. Pattern generalizes across all 3 tested datasets.}
492:In contrast, \textbf{RR-MoA} maintains healthy routing entropy ($1.0$--$1.57$) and wins \textbf{54/54} configurations, with MSE improvements of $26\%$ to $79\%$ over the best fixed adapter. Notably, on Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, where the stripped statistics carry little information), RR-MoA does not improve over fixed adapters, exactly as Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}).
494:\textbf{Rescue-baseline sweep.} To rule out that the collapse is merely under-tuned, we sweep five standard MoE rescue families (Switch load-balance~\citep{fedus2022switch}, ST-MoE z-loss~\citep{zoph2022stmoe}, entropy regularization, ReMoE ReLU routing~\citep{zhang2025remoe}, and expert-choice routing~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice}) across eleven configurations on 6 datasets $\times$ 2 freeze levels $\times$ 5 seeds $= 720$ runs (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}, Appendix~\ref{app:rescue_details}). The best rescue (z-loss $c_z{=}0.01$) reduces MSE by only $10.9\%$, still $2.7{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA. Excessive balancing ($\alpha{=}10.0$) actively hurts MSE ($+67.7\%$). This confirms Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(ii): when routing information is structurally absent from the hidden states, none of the standard rescue mechanisms we tested can compensate.
498:\textbf{The principle is architecture-agnostic (SR-MoA).} To confirm the bottleneck is in the routing \emph{input} rather than the router \emph{architecture}, SR-MoA~\citep{lv2025aoe,cheng2025ermoe,liu2026routingfree} replaces the external Conv1d router with a per-expert sigmoid gate $\sigma_k(\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw})$, with no external router at all. SR-MoA \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets (ETTh1: $0.464$; ETTm1: $0.388$; Weather: $0.222$; 252 runs total), generalizes across six backbones, and independently replicates the Frozen Paradox (frozen best on 4/6 datasets; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). That a different architecture exceeds RR-MoA while sharing only the raw-input principle confirms it is the \emph{principle}, not the specific router design, that drives the gains. The same SR-MoA architecture gating on hidden states degrades by $+87\%$ to $+768\%$ on \textbf{all 6 datasets} ($p{=}0.016$, Wilcoxon). \textbf{Normalization-specificity:} on Timer-XL (no RevIN), the same raw-vs-hidden comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$ ($15$--$100{\times}$ smaller than on MOMENT), confirming the degradation is normalization-induced. A third architecture-level control, \textbf{AdaMix-Raw} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}), replaces AdaMix's hidden-state router with a raw-input router and recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells. All three router instantiations (RR-MoA, SR-MoA, AdaMix-Raw) are consistent with the same diagnosis: the bottleneck is normalization, not router design. Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls} summarizes all eight controls.
524:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why the diagnosis matters more than the architectural label.} The MoE community has framed routing collapse as both an \emph{optimization} problem (load balancing, z-loss all attack training dynamics; effective when router input is informative but under-optimized) and a \emph{post-training representation} problem~\citep{chi2022representation,hua2025inputaware}, where token features cluster around expert centroids. Our setting is upstream of both: an \emph{architectural inductive bias} (RevIN) strips the per-window statistics that carry the routing signal \emph{before training starts}. Optimization-side rescue mechanisms cannot recover what is structurally absent (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}), and post-training representation fixes presuppose information the input never carried. The mechanism decomposes into two distinct gains: \emph{(Q1)} the best fixed adapter on the same RevIN-normalized hidden states still loses to RR-MoA by $26$--$79\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa}), so residual shape heterogeneity survives normalization and rewards diverse experts; \emph{(Q2)} the signal-ratio $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ measures \emph{dispatch}-signal localization in the stripped statistics, which is why dense routing also benefits from raw access and why Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) correctly does not improve. The Pure Raw-MLP MoE ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:raw_mlp_moe}) makes this concrete: when the router goes to near-uniform on raw input, the architecture behaves as a raw-input ensemble, sufficient on Weather/ETTm2/Electricity (DLinear regime) but insufficient on ETT-temperature data where the TSFM contributes complementary nonlinear structure. The durable contribution is the diagnosis, not the architectural label; the choice between ``MoE with raw router'' and ``ensemble with raw input'' is dataset-conditional, we claim only that wherever instance normalization sits between input and router, the routing signal needs to be re-injected from upstream.}
